< [2026-11-07T10:44:46Z] checkpoint of #341 (a step) pushed to looper/tacplus-341:  6 files changed, 613 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)
I fixed the test, but nothing has merged to main yet: the land has been queued since 07:33 and had no verdict when I stopped for time. The manager will finish the land after this run. I haven't tagged v2.6.2, opened backport PRs, commented on #341 or closed it, so the next run has to do those.

**Cause:** the generated Go spool enforces its disk bound correctly. It counts the log's bytes in memory, so the filesystem's reported size plays no part. Every append fsyncs to disk, and about 16 records fit under the 1024-byte test bound. The test waited a fixed 1s and then read the drop count. On the runner's busy disk those 16 fsyncs took longer than 1s, so it read zero drops. The CI failure took 1.11s, which is that 1s wait plus the test's 100ms sleep.

**Fix (43b78a62):** only the Go and Java test files change; the generator stays as it is.
- **Disk-bound test:** it now waits until every emitted record is either in the log or counted as dropped. Then it checks that some were dropped and that the log on disk is at most 1024 bytes.
- **Other waits:** every wait on fsync'd work (appends, cursor writes, cleanup, replay) now uses a 60s limit that only catches hangs. The 1s limit still applies to the RPC side.
- **Rust:** no change. Its appends run in the same thread as its wait loop, so it already waits for them.

**Testing:**
- **Reproduced the failure:** I added a 100ms delay before each fsync in a scratch copy of the generated code. The old Go test then fails with CI's exact message (line 196, 1.10s), and a second Go test also fails. The old Java test fails too.
- **New tests:** with the same delay, they pass in both languages.
- **Bound check:** with the size limit disabled in the generated code, the new Go test fails, so it does pin the bound.
- **Local gates:** `just gates` in the build image passed protoc-check, soundness and test. In current-generation, the Go, Java and Rust accounting-sink fixtures passed and nothing had failed when I stopped it for time. So I don't have a complete local run, and the land's own full gates are still pending.

**Release plan for the next run:**
- **Patch:** main has only planning-file commits since v2.6.1, so v2.6.2 can be tagged on main once the land's full gates are green.
- **Backports:** the fix applies cleanly to release/2.1 through 2.6. On release/2.1 the Go and Java durable tests pass; release/2.2–2.6 have the same spool templates as main.
- **Patch number clash:** open PR #326 had planned v2.6.2 for release/2.6, so its patch becomes v2.6.3.

**Also done:**
- **#351 filed:** the record-egress gate's check for the retired `linked` claim discards every match under the gate's own `target/` directory, so it can never fail. Run outside that directory, it flags the reserved name protoc-gen-go embeds in `token.pb.go`, a false positive.
- **Follow-up in PROPOSALS.md (d01d873d):** audit other self-tests that wait on disk writes against a fixed time limit.
- **Trace check:** no handlers changed. Its 9 findings are all already filed as #310 and #311.

The final checkpoint on looper/tacplus-341 lists the remaining steps.
